{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Dinah daughter of Leah went out.</b> Jacob was punished in the matter of Dinah for several reasons. The first, because of what he said to Laban (Genesis 30:33): ״וענתה בי צדקתי ביום מחר״ — 'and my righteousness shall answer for me tomorrow.' The second, because he withheld Dinah from Esau his brother, for perhaps she would have turned him back to the better. The third, because he took authority to himself. And this is what they said in Bereshit Rabbah: the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him (Proverbs 27:1): ״אל תתהלל ביום מחר״ — 'Do not boast about tomorrow' — you said ״וענתה בי צדקתי״ and so on; tomorrow your daughter goes out and is afflicted." "<b>And furthermore</b> there [in Bereshit Rabbah]: ״ויקח את שתי נשיו ואת שתי שפחותיו ואת אחד עשר ילדיו״ — and where was Dinah? This teaches that Jacob put her into a box and locked her inside. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: You did not want her to marry a circumcised man — she will marry an uncircumcised one; you did not want her to marry in permitted fashion — she will marry in forbidden fashion; tomorrow your daughter goes out and is afflicted." "<b>And furthermore</b> there: ״ויקרא לו אל״ and so on. [Jacob] said to Him: You are the God of the beings above, and I am the god of the beings below. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Even a cantor in a synagogue does not take authority to himself, yet you took authority to yourself — tomorrow your daughter goes out and is afflicted. Therefore Scripture juxtaposes ״ותצא״. End." "<b>It appears</b> that there [in the Midrash] they understood that Jacob is the one saying this to the Holy One, blessed be He, and therefore He punished him immediately in the matter of Dinah for having taken authority to himself." "<b>And the matter of</b> 'and Dinah went out' teaches you that she went out adorned, just as her mother Leah went out, of whom Scripture says (Genesis 30:16): ״ותצא לאה לקראתו״. That is to say: a woman who goes out — a woman who goes out. And Scripture disparages a woman when she is 'one who goes out,' as it is written (Proverbs 7:11–12): ״הומיה היא וסוררת בביתה לא ישכנו רגליה, פעם בחוץ פעם ברחובות״ — 'She is clamorous and rebellious; her feet do not abide in her house; now outside, now in the streets.' But when she is modest and stays in her house, Scripture praises her, as it is said (Psalms 45:14): ״כל כבודה בת מלך פנימה״ — 'All glorious is the king's daughter within.' And likewise it is written of Sarah our mother (Genesis 18): ״הנה באהל״ — 'she is in the tent.'" "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> When a woman stands within her house she atones for her household just as the altar atones for the earth, as it is written (Psalms 128:3): ״אשתך כגפן פוריה בירכתי ביתך״ — your wife like a fruitful vine in the innermost chambers of your house. It is written here 'בירכתי ביתך' — the innermost chambers of your house, and it is written there concerning the altar (Leviticus 1:11): ״ושחט אותו על ירך המזבח״ — and slaughter it on the side of the altar. If she does so, she will raise sons worthy to be anointed with the anointing oil, as it says: ״בניך כשתילי זיתים״ — your sons like olive saplings." "<b>And so did the Rabbis of blessed memory expound</b> in tractate Yoma, at the beginning of the chapter 'They brought out for him': There was an incident involving Kimḥit, who had seven sons and all of them served in the high priesthood during her lifetime. They said to her: What did you do to merit this? She said to them: Throughout my days, the beams of my house have never seen the braids of my hair. And the exposition of ״אשתך כגפן פוריה״ — your wife like a fruitful vine: he likened the modest woman to a vine, because the custom is to plant the vine in the innermost part of the house, and when it grows large they bring its branches out through a hole in the house to the outside, toward the sun; and so the root stands within the house and its branches are outside the house. So too the modest woman is fit to sit in the innermost chambers of the house, not at the entrance of the house, lest passersby see her — in the manner that Solomon, peace be upon him, mentioned (Proverbs 9:14): ״ושבה לפתח ביתה״ — and she sits at the entrance of her house. And since she stands in the innermost chambers of the house while her sons go outside to the field for their work, these sons are like olive saplings. And he likened them to olives because there is no tree that does not accept grafting except the olive tree, which does not accept grafting at all; and so too the sons are truly their father's sons — for she was modest in the innermost chambers of the house and did not consort with any but her husband. And further he likened them to the olive for another reason, namely that the olive bears its fruit after nine months, and so this woman, in reward for her modesty, does not miscarry but gives birth to her sons after nine months. And so they expounded, of blessed memory, in Bekhorot: a large pure animal [carries for] nine months, and corresponding to it among trees is the olive. And so you will find in the ascending degrees, which are fifteen, that this verse appears in the ninth degree. 'סביב לשלחנך' — around your table — that is, they will be ready to serve you when you eat." "<b>And further:</b> It is known that all trees have a heart when you cut them, but the olive tree has no heart, and perhaps for this reason the nature of its fruit causes forgetting of learning and dulling of the heart, as our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned concerning the olive." "<b>And there is one who explained</b> further: ״אשתך כגפן פוריה״ — that he is speaking of the time of her menstruation, when she sees blood, and therefore it says ״כגפן פוריה״; and it says ״בירכתי ביתך״ — the innermost chambers of your house — meaning that she should be distanced from you to another side, just as you keep away from fire. And from this it says 'אשתך' with a segol and not with a ḥiriq. And if you do so, then your sons will be like olive saplings — they will be worthy and there will be in them no trace of disqualification." "<b>To see the daughters of the land.</b> She came to see the daughters of the land, who were dancing in the dances, in the manner of (Judges 21:21): ״והיה אם יצאו בנות שילה לחול במחולות״." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> To see — she went out to see and was seen. And Hamor father of Shechem went out to Jacob to speak with him. They said in the Midrash: he said to him, I know that Abraham your grandfather was a prince, as it is said (Genesis 23:6): ״נשיא אלהים אתה בתוכנו״, and I am prince of the land — give a prince's daughter to a prince. Jacob said to him: 'prince' is nothing but an ox, as it is said (Genesis 18:7): ״ואל הבקר רץ אברהם״, and it is written (Proverbs 14:4): ״ורב תבואות בכח שור״, and you are a donkey, and they cannot be yoked together, for the Torah said: ״לא תחרוש בשור ובחמור יחדו״, and the prophet cried out against your wickedness — that which you seek, as it is said (II Kings 14:9): ״החוח אשר בלבנון שלח אל הארז אשר בלבנון לאמר תנה את בתך לבני לאשה ותעבור חית השדה אשר בלבנון ותרמוס את החוח״. And ״ותעבור חית השדה״ — these are the sons of Jacob, who were likened to beasts: Judah — ״גור אריה״; Dan — ״יהי דן נחש״; Benjamin — ״זאב יטרף״; Naphtali — ״אילה שלוחה״. ״ותרמוס את החוח״ — ״ויבואו על העיר בטח ויהרגו כל זכר״. And so the prophet cried out (Hosea 6:9): ״וכחכי איש גדודים חבר כהנים דרך ירצחו שכמה״. And why? ״כי זמה עשו״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {3 "The pronoun 'there' (שם) refers back to the Midrashic passage cited in comment 2; 'this' refers to the statement attributed to Jacob.", 8 "Hebrew 'בסגו\"ל ולא בחירי\"ק' refers to the vocalization of the word אשתך; with segol it reads 'your wife,' with ḥiriq it would read 'your women.' The point is grammatical, likely tied to halakhic distancing.", 10 "The comment lacks one of Rabbeinu Bahya's standard four method-headers; it opens directly with a Midrash citation. The phrase 'לראות, יצאה לראות ונראית' appears to gloss the immediately preceding lemma from the verse (ותצא דינה... לראות) even though the bolded lemma here is ובמדרש."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:02.341375089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}